TOS (Tiny OS) -- Kernel

MINIX-style preemptive scheduler with per-priority run queues.

Core rule: always run the highest-priority runnable thread.
Within the same priority, threads are scheduled round-robin
using FIFO queues with time quantum expiry.

Priority convention: lower number = higher priority (0 is highest).

Features:
- Per-priority FIFO run queues with ready mask
- Time quantum with round-robin rotation
- Thread statistics (context switches, CPU ticks)
- Watchdog (kills threads exceeding max consecutive quanta)
- Software timers (one-shot callbacks after N ticks)

All kernel services are accessed through trap 0 (syscall).
The trap handler in boot.asm dispatches based on r1:
- 0 = sleep(ticks) -- block for N timer ticks
- 1 = putc(char) -- write character to stdout
- 2 = yield() -- voluntary context switch


    module oskit.kernel

    import oskit.config.{MAX_THREADS, NUM_PRIORITIES, DEFAULT_QUANTUM, THREAD_STACK_SIZE, THREAD_STACK_HALF, PAGE_SIZE, KERNEL_L1_BASE, PAGE_POOL_BASE, PAGE_POOL_END, MAX_SYSCALLS, TLS_SLOTS, MAX_TIMERS, MAX_IRQS, MAX_PROCESSES}

Kernel thread stacks. A static array in BSS, sized at compile
time. Each thread gets THREAD_STACK_SIZE bytes, split evenly
between user stack (lower half) and supervisor stack (upper
half). Stacks grow downward, so USP and SSP point to the TOP
of each half.

Placing stacks in BSS guarantees they cannot be overwritten
by sbrk (heap grows from _heap_start, which is after BSS).
This follows the Minix 3 approach of static kernel task stacks.

    var kernel_stacks: [8][16384]byte  // 8 = MAX_THREADS, 16384 = THREAD_STACK_SIZE

Return (usp, ssp) for a thread slot. USP is the top of the
lower half; SSP is the top of the upper half. The caller
passes these to create_thread.

    kernel_stack_usp(slot: int) -> i64
        i64(&kernel_stacks[slot][0]) + THREAD_STACK_HALF

    kernel_stack_ssp(slot: int) -> i64
        i64(&kernel_stacks[slot][0]) + THREAD_STACK_SIZE

    val STATE_READY        = 0
    val STATE_RUNNING      = 1
    val STATE_BLOCKED      = 2
    val STATE_TERMINATED   = 3
    val STATE_JOINING      = 4
    val STATE_SUSPENDED    = 5
    val STATE_SEND_BLOCKED        = 20
    val STATE_RECV_BLOCKED        = 21
    val STATE_RECV_NOTIFY_BLOCKED = 22

    val QUEUE_EMPTY = -1
    val STACK_CANARY = 0xDEAD

Thread struct field order matters: ssp must remain at offset 0
because boot.asm accesses it directly when saving/restoring
context.

The next field links threads in the same priority run queue.
QUEUE_EMPTY (-1) marks the end of a queue.

Statistics fields track per-thread performance:
- ctx_switches: number of times this thread was scheduled in
- cpu_ticks: total ticks spent running
- consec_quanta: consecutive full quanta without voluntary yield/sleep


    struct Thread
        ssp: i64
        state: int
        wake_tick: int
        name: string
        priority: int
        join_target: int
        quantum: int
        next: int
        ctx_switches: int
        cpu_ticks: int
        consec_quanta: int
        notify_value: int
        notify_pending: int
        base_priority: int
        uid: int
        blocked_on: i64
        ptbr: int
        pid: int

Run queues: one FIFO queue per priority level. Each queue
has a head and tail index. Enqueue appends to tail, dequeue
removes from head.

ready_mask is a bitmask: bit i is set iff queue i is non-empty.
Finding the highest-priority non-empty queue is a single scan
from bit 0.

    var queue_head: [4]int
    var queue_tail: [4]int
    var ready_mask = 0
    var queues_initialized = 0

    var threads: [8]Thread
    var current_thread = -1
    var thread_count = 0
    var ticks = 0

Process table. A process wraps one or more threads with an
isolated address space (page table). PID 0 is the kernel;
all kernel threads implicitly belong to it. External programs
loaded by nsh each get their own process entry.

    val PROC_FREE    = 0
    val PROC_RUNNING = 1
    val PROC_ZOMBIE  = 2

    struct Process
        state: int
        ptbr: int
        parent_tid: int
        exit_code: int
        main_tid: int
        name: string

    var processes: [16]Process  // 16 = MAX_PROCESSES
    var next_pid = 1

PM registration. The PM server registers its thread ID so
the kernel can send notifications on process exit.

    var kernel_pm_tid = -1

    kernel_register_pm(tid: int)
        kernel_pm_tid = tid

Watchdog configuration. When a thread uses max_consec_quanta
consecutive full quanta without voluntarily yielding or sleeping,
the watchdog terminates it. Set to 0 to disable.

    var watchdog_limit = 0
    var total_ctx_switches = 0

Idle and tick hooks. Function pointers called by the scheduler.
idle_hook is called when no threads are ready (once per idle entry).
tick_hook is called on every timer tick. Set to 0 to disable.

    var idle_hook = 0
    var tick_hook = 0

Sleep queue: a fixed-size min-heap of thread indices ordered
by wake_tick. Replaces the O(n) scan in schedule() with O(log n)
insert and O(1) peek. Uses lazy deletion — when a thread is
popped but is no longer blocked, it's discarded.

No allocator dependency: uses a static array sized to MAX_THREADS.

    private var _sq_data: [8]int
    private var _sq_len = 0

    private _sq_swap(a: int, b: int)
        val tmp = _sq_data[a]
        _sq_data[a] = _sq_data[b]
        _sq_data[b] = tmp

    private _sq_sift_up(i: int)
        var pos = i
        while pos > 0
            val parent = (pos - 1) / 2
            if threads[_sq_data[pos]].wake_tick < threads[_sq_data[parent]].wake_tick
                _sq_swap(pos, parent)
                pos = parent
            else
                return

    private _sq_sift_down(i: int)
        var pos = i
        while true
            val left = 2 * pos + 1
            val right = 2 * pos + 2
            var smallest = pos
            if left < _sq_len
                if threads[_sq_data[left]].wake_tick < threads[_sq_data[smallest]].wake_tick
                    smallest = left
            if right < _sq_len
                if threads[_sq_data[right]].wake_tick < threads[_sq_data[smallest]].wake_tick
                    smallest = right
            if smallest == pos
                return
            _sq_swap(pos, smallest)
            pos = smallest

Insert a thread into the sleep queue. Called when a thread
blocks with a wake_tick deadline (sleep, IPC timeout).

    sleepq_insert(tid: int)
        _sq_data[_sq_len] = tid
        _sq_len += 1
        _sq_sift_up(_sq_len - 1)

    private _sq_peek() -> int = _sq_data[0]

    private _sq_pop() -> int
        val min = _sq_data[0]
        _sq_len -= 1
        _sq_data[0] = _sq_data[_sq_len]
        if _sq_len > 0
            _sq_sift_down(0)
        min

    val STATE_NOTIFY_WAIT = 10
    val STATE_MUTEX_WAIT = 11

Syscall dispatch table. Each slot holds a function pointer
(i64) for a slow-path syscall handler. Handlers receive one
arg (int) and are called with context already saved. After
the handler returns, the scheduler runs.

Modules register handlers via register_syscall(). Fast-path
syscalls (putc, queries) remain hardcoded in boot.asm.

    var syscall_table: [48]i64  // 48 = MAX_SYSCALLS
    var syscall_ssp: i64

Set the return value for the current syscall. Writes into
the saved r1 slot on the SSP so the caller sees it after
context restore.

    syscall_return(value: i64)
        var p: *i64 = *i64(syscall_ssp + 48)
        *p = value

Write a return value into a specific thread's saved context.
Used by the scheduler for generic timeout wakeups.

    syscall_return_for(tid: int, value: i64)
        var p: *i64 = *i64(threads[tid].ssp + 48)
        *p = value

    register_syscall(num: int, handler: i64)
        if num >= 0
            if num < MAX_SYSCALLS
                syscall_table[num] = handler

MMU page table constants. Two-level page tables with 4KB pages.
Superpages: an L1 entry with R/W/X bits maps 4MB directly.

PTE format (32-bit): [PPN (20 bits) | reserved (2) | flags (10)]
PTE flags: V=0x001, R=0x002, W=0x004, X=0x008, U=0x010, G=0x020

    val PTE_V = 0x001
    val PTE_R = 0x002
    val PTE_W = 0x004
    val PTE_X = 0x008
    val PTE_U = 0x010
    val PTE_G = 0x020

Kernel L1 page table at 0x7FE000, page pool at 0x620000-0x7FD000.

    // Initialized lazily in page_alloc() because sysl codegen cannot yet
    // resolve cross-unit constants in module-level var initializers.
    var page_pool_next: int = 0
    var page_pool_init = false

    vm_set_ptbr(addr: int)
        asm("sptbr r1, r0")

    vm_flush_tlb()
        asm("tlbia r0, r0")

    vm_get_ptbr() -> int
        asm("gptbr r1, r0")
        0

Build kernel identity page table using superpages (4MB each):
- L1[0]: 0x000000-0x3FFFFF (RAM low) — supervisor RWX
- L1[1]: 0x400000-0x7FFFFF (RAM high) — supervisor RWX
- L1[2]: 0x800000-0xBFFFFF (I/O + ramdisk) — supervisor RW (no execute)

    vm_init()
        val flags_rwx = PTE_V | PTE_R | PTE_W | PTE_X
        val flags_rw  = PTE_V | PTE_R | PTE_W

        // Zero the L1 table (1024 entries × 4 bytes = 4096 bytes)
        memset(*byte(KERNEL_L1_BASE), 0, PAGE_SIZE)

        // L1[0]: superpage PPN=0, maps 0x000000-0x3FFFFF
        var p0: *i32 = *i32(KERNEL_L1_BASE)
        *p0 = i32((0 << 10) | flags_rwx)

        // L1[1]: superpage PPN=1, maps 0x400000-0x7FFFFF
        var p1: *i32 = *i32(KERNEL_L1_BASE + 4)
        *p1 = i32((1 << 10) | flags_rwx)

        // L1[2]: superpage PPN=2, maps 0x800000-0xBFFFFF
        var p2: *i32 = *i32(KERNEL_L1_BASE + 8)
        *p2 = i32((2 << 10) | flags_rw)

        // Enable the MMU
        vm_set_ptbr(KERNEL_L1_BASE)

Physical page allocator — bump allocator with free list.
Freed pages are pushed onto a singly-linked list (next pointer
stored in the first 8 bytes of each free page). page_alloc
pops from the free list first, then falls back to bumping.

    import oskit.hal.memset

    var page_free_list = 0

    page_alloc() -> int
        if !page_pool_init
            page_pool_next = PAGE_POOL_BASE
            page_pool_init = true
        if page_free_list != 0
            val page = page_free_list
            var next: *i64 = *i64(page)
            page_free_list = int(*next)
            return page
        if page_pool_next >= PAGE_POOL_END
            return 0
        val page = page_pool_next
        page_pool_next += PAGE_SIZE
        page

    page_free(addr: int)
        var p: *i64 = *i64(addr)
        *p = i64(page_free_list)
        page_free_list = addr

Allocate a zeroed page (for page tables and BSS).

    page_alloc_zero() -> int
        val page = page_alloc()
        if page != 0
            memset(*byte(page), 0, PAGE_SIZE)
        page

Write a 32-bit PTE at a given address.

    vm_write_pte(addr: int, pte: int)
        var p: *i32 = *i32(addr)
        *p = i32(pte)

Create a per-process page table. Allocates L1 + L2 tables and
physical pages for the program region (0xC0000-0xFFFFF = 64 pages).
Returns L1 base address, or 0 on failure.

    vm_create_process_pt() -> int
        val l1 = page_alloc_zero()
        if l1 == 0
            return 0
        val l2 = page_alloc_zero()
        if l2 == 0
            return 0

        val flags_rwx = PTE_V | PTE_R | PTE_W | PTE_X
        val flags_rw  = PTE_V | PTE_R | PTE_W

        // L1[0] = pointer to L2 (V-only, no R/W/X → non-superpage)
        val l2_ppn = l2 / PAGE_SIZE
        vm_write_pte(l1, (l2_ppn << 10) | PTE_V)

        // L1[1] and L1[2] = copy kernel superpages
        var k1: *i32 = *i32(KERNEL_L1_BASE + 4)
        vm_write_pte(l1 + 4, *k1)
        var k2: *i32 = *i32(KERNEL_L1_BASE + 8)
        vm_write_pte(l1 + 8, *k2)

        // L2[0x00-0xCB]: kernel region, identity-mapped supervisor RWX
        // Covers up to 0xCBFFF (835KB). Programs start at PROG_SSP=0xCC000.
        var i = 0
        while i < 0xCC
            var p: *i32 = *i32(l2 + i * 4)
            *p = i32((i << 10) | flags_rwx)
            i += 1

        // L2[0xCC-0xFF]: program region, fresh physical pages
        // Matches PROG_SSP=0xCC000, PROG_USP=0xCE000, PROG_BASE=0xD0000
        while i < 0x100
            val page = page_alloc()
            if page == 0
                return 0
            val ppn = page / PAGE_SIZE
            var p: *i32 = *i32(l2 + i * 4)
            *p = i32((ppn << 10) | flags_rwx)
            i += 1

        // L2[0x100-0x3FF]: unmapped (already zeroed by page_alloc)
        l1

Free all pages allocated for a process page table: the program
region pages (L2 entries 0xCC-0xFF), the L2 table, and the L1
table. Kernel identity-mapped pages (L2 entries 0x00-0xCB) are
NOT freed — they point to shared kernel memory.

    vm_free_process_pt(l1_addr: int)
        if l1_addr == 0
            return
        if l1_addr == KERNEL_L1_BASE
            return

        // Read L1[0] to find the L2 table
        var l1e: *i32 = *i32(l1_addr)
        val l1_pte = *l1e
        if (l1_pte & PTE_V) == 0
            return
        val l2_addr = ((l1_pte >> 10) & 0xFFFFF) << 12

        // Free program pages (L2 entries 0xCC-0xFF)
        var i = 0xCC
        while i < 0x100
            var p: *i32 = *i32(l2_addr + i * 4)
            val pte = *p
            if (pte & PTE_V) != 0
                val phys = ((pte >> 10) & 0xFFFFF) << 12
                page_free(phys)
            i += 1

        // Free the L2 and L1 tables themselves
        page_free(l2_addr)
        page_free(l1_addr)

Software page table walk — translate a virtual address using
the given page table. Used for cross-address-space copies.

    vm_v2p(ptbr: int, vaddr: int) -> int
        val l1_idx = (vaddr >> 22) & 0x3FF
        val l2_idx = (vaddr >> 12) & 0x3FF
        val offset = vaddr & 0xFFF

        var l1e: *i32 = *i32(ptbr + l1_idx * 4)
        val l1_pte = *l1e
        val l1_flags = l1_pte & 0x3FF

        // Superpage?
        if (l1_flags & (PTE_R | PTE_W | PTE_X)) != 0
            val ppn = (l1_pte >> 10) & 0xFFFFF
            return (ppn << 22) | (vaddr & 0x3FFFFF)

        // L2 walk
        val l2_base = (l1_pte >> 10) << 12
        var l2e: *i32 = *i32(l2_base + l2_idx * 4)
        val l2_pte = *l2e
        val ppn = (l2_pte >> 10) & 0xFFFFF
        (ppn << 12) | offset

Copy data into another address space using physical addressing.
Translates the destination virtual address to physical via
software page table walk, then copies using the identity-mapped
physical address. No PTBR switching needed.

    import oskit.hal.memcpy

    vm_copy_to(dst_ptbr: int, dst_vaddr: int, src: *byte, len: int)
        var copied = 0
        while copied < len
            val pa = vm_v2p(dst_ptbr, dst_vaddr + copied)
            val page_off = (dst_vaddr + copied) & 0xFFF
            var chunk = PAGE_SIZE - page_off
            if chunk > len - copied
                chunk = len - copied
            memcpy(*byte(pa), src + copied, chunk)
            copied += chunk

Copy data from another address space using physical addressing.

    vm_copy_from(src_ptbr: int, src_vaddr: int, dst: *byte, len: int)
        var copied = 0
        while copied < len
            val pa = vm_v2p(src_ptbr, src_vaddr + copied)
            val page_off = (src_vaddr + copied) & 0xFFF
            var chunk = PAGE_SIZE - page_off
            if chunk > len - copied
                chunk = len - copied
            memcpy(dst + copied, *byte(pa), chunk)
            copied += chunk

Get a thread's page table base register.

    kernel_get_thread_ptbr(tid: int) -> int
        threads[tid].ptbr

Set a thread's page table base register.

    kernel_set_thread_ptbr(tid: int, ptbr: int)
        threads[tid].ptbr = ptbr

    kernel_get_thread_pid(tid: int) -> int
        threads[tid].pid

    kernel_init()
        vm_init()
        register_syscall(0, sleep_current)
        register_syscall(2, yield_current)
        register_syscall(3, terminate_current)
        register_syscall(4, join_current)
        register_syscall(10, sleep_until_current)
        register_syscall(29, pimutex_lock_kernel)
        register_syscall(30, pimutex_unlock_kernel)
        register_syscall(40, waitpid_current)
        register_syscall(41, getpid_current)

Thread-local storage. A flat 2D array indexed by [thread_id][slot].
Each thread gets TLS_SLOTS slots of int-sized values. Access is
O(1) via tls_set/tls_get kernel functions.

    var tls: [32]int  // 32 = MAX_THREADS * TLS_SLOTS

Software timers. A fixed pool of one-shot timers that fire a
callback function after a specified number of ticks. The timer
queue is scanned each tick in schedule().

Each timer has:
- active: 1 if armed, 0 if free
- fire_tick: absolute tick when the timer fires
- callback: function pointer to call (unit -> unit)


    struct SoftTimer
        active: int
        fire_tick: int
        callback: i64

    var timers: [8]SoftTimer  // 8 = MAX_TIMERS
    var irq_handlers: [8]i64  // 8 = MAX_IRQS

    extern thread_exit()

    register_irq(irq: int, handler: i64)
        irq_handlers[irq] = handler

ensure_queues_init -- Lazily initialize queues on first use.
Global arrays default to 0 but queues need QUEUE_EMPTY (-1).

    ensure_queues_init()
        if queues_initialized == 0
            init_queues()
            queues_initialized = 1

Queue operations -- enqueue, dequeue, and remove from run queues.
These maintain the ready_mask invariant.

    enqueue(pri: int, idx: int)
        threads[idx].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
        if queue_head[pri] == QUEUE_EMPTY
            queue_head[pri] = idx
            queue_tail[pri] = idx
        else
            threads[queue_tail[pri]].next = idx
            queue_tail[pri] = idx
        ready_mask = ready_mask | (1 << pri)

    dequeue(pri: int) -> int
        val idx = queue_head[pri]
        if idx == QUEUE_EMPTY
            return QUEUE_EMPTY
        queue_head[pri] = threads[idx].next
        if queue_head[pri] == QUEUE_EMPTY
            queue_tail[pri] = QUEUE_EMPTY
            ready_mask = ready_mask & ~(1 << pri)
        threads[idx].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
        idx

    init_queues()
        var i = 0
        while i < NUM_PRIORITIES
            queue_head[i] = QUEUE_EMPTY
            queue_tail[i] = QUEUE_EMPTY
            i += 1
        ready_mask = 0

build_stack_frame -- Build the initial context frame for a new thread.
The frame must match the layout that boot.asm expects:
PSR, PC, r1-r6, USP (9 x 8 bytes, top to bottom)

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        var sp = ssp
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 2                 // PSR: Mode=1 (supervisor), Ind=0 (interrupts on)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = entry_point       // PC
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r1
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r2
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r3
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r4
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = 0                 // r5
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = thread_exit       // r6 = return address (exit trampoline)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = usp               // USP
        sp

    create_thread(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, name: string) -> int =
        create_thread_pri(entry_point, usp, ssp, name, 0, KERNEL_L1_BASE)

Create a thread with a specific page table. The ptbr is set
atomically before the thread is enqueued, preventing a race
where the scheduler picks up the thread with the wrong ptbr.

    create_thread_vm(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, name: string, ptbr: int) -> int =
        create_thread_pri(entry_point, usp, ssp, name, 0, ptbr)

Lower priority number = higher priority. Priority 0 runs first.

Allocates a thread slot: reuses the lowest-index STATE_TERMINATED slot if any,
otherwise appends at thread_count (if below MAX_THREADS). Returns the thread
index, or -1 if every slot is in use. Without reuse, short-lived /bin programs
would monotonically bump thread_count and walk off the end of threads[].

    create_thread_pri(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, name: string, priority: int, ptbr_val: int) -> int
        asm("cli")
        ensure_queues_init()
        var idx = -1
        var i = 0
        while i < thread_count
            if threads[i].state == STATE_TERMINATED
                idx = i
                break
            i += 1
        if idx < 0
            if thread_count >= MAX_THREADS
                asm("sti")
                return -1
            idx = thread_count
            thread_count += 1
        // Build stack frame with the child's PTBR so writes go to the
        // correct physical pages (SSP is in program-mapped region)
        if ptbr_val != KERNEL_L1_BASE
            vm_set_ptbr(ptbr_val)
        val sp = build_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, ssp)
        if ptbr_val != KERNEL_L1_BASE
            vm_set_ptbr(KERNEL_L1_BASE)
        threads[idx].ssp = sp
        threads[idx].state = STATE_READY
        threads[idx].wake_tick = 0
        threads[idx].name = name
        threads[idx].priority = priority
        threads[idx].join_target = -1
        threads[idx].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
        threads[idx].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
        threads[idx].ctx_switches = 0
        threads[idx].cpu_ticks = 0
        threads[idx].consec_quanta = 0
        threads[idx].notify_value = 0
        threads[idx].notify_pending = 0
        threads[idx].base_priority = priority
        threads[idx].blocked_on = 0
        threads[idx].ptbr = ptbr_val
        threads[idx].pid = 0
        if current_thread >= 0
            threads[idx].uid = threads[current_thread].uid
        else
            threads[idx].uid = 0
        // Write stack canary at bottom of user stack (skip for process threads
        // where USP overlaps with code base)
        if ptbr_val == KERNEL_L1_BASE
            var canary_addr: *int = *int(usp)
            *canary_addr = STACK_CANARY
        enqueue(priority, idx)
        asm("sti")
        idx

    sleep_current(nticks: int)
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_BLOCKED
        threads[current_thread].wake_tick = ticks + nticks
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0
        sleepq_insert(current_thread)

    sleep_until_current(target_tick: int)
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_BLOCKED
        threads[current_thread].wake_tick = target_tick
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0
        sleepq_insert(current_thread)

    yield_current()
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0
        threads[current_thread].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM

    query_thread_state(id: int) -> int
        threads[id].state

    query_thread_name(id: int) -> *byte
        *byte(threads[id].name)

    query_thread_name_len(id: int) -> int
        len(threads[id].name)

    kernel_getuid() -> int
        threads[current_thread].uid

    kernel_setuid(uid: int)
        threads[current_thread].uid = uid

    query_thread_pid(id: int) -> int
        threads[id].pid

    query_thread_ctx_switches(id: int) -> int
        threads[id].ctx_switches

    query_thread_cpu_ticks(id: int) -> int
        threads[id].cpu_ticks

    query_total_ctx_switches() -> int
        total_ctx_switches

    kernel_set_watchdog(limit: int)
        watchdog_limit = limit

Also wakes any threads that are joining on this thread,
enqueueing them back into their priority run queue.

    terminate_current(exit_code: int)
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_TERMINATED

        // If this thread belongs to a process, mark it as zombie
        // and notify PM so it can handle reaping
        val pid = threads[current_thread].pid
        if pid > 0
            processes[pid].exit_code = exit_code
            processes[pid].state = PROC_ZOMBIE
            if kernel_pm_tid >= 0
                notify_send(kernel_pm_tid, pid)

        // Wake threads waiting to join on this thread
        // (used for kernel threads; processes use PM)
        var i = 0
        while i < thread_count
            if threads[i].state == STATE_JOINING
                if threads[i].join_target == current_thread
                    threads[i].state = STATE_READY
                    threads[i].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
                    enqueue(threads[i].priority, i)
            i += 1

If the target is already terminated, returns immediately without
blocking. Otherwise sets state to STATE_JOINING and records the
target thread ID so terminate_current can wake us.

    join_current(target_id: int)
        if threads[target_id].state == STATE_TERMINATED
            return
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_JOINING
        threads[current_thread].join_target = target_id
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0

Process management. create_process allocates a process table
entry and creates a thread for it. waitpid blocks the caller
until the process exits. getpid returns the current thread's
process ID (0 for kernel threads).

    create_process(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, name: string, ptbr_val: int) -> int
        // Allocate PID
        var pid = -1
        var i = 1
        while i < MAX_PROCESSES
            if processes[i].state == PROC_FREE
                pid = i
                break
            i += 1
        if pid < 0
            return -1

        // Create the thread
        val tid = create_thread_pri(entry_point, usp, ssp, name, 0, ptbr_val)
        if tid < 0
            return -1

        // Set up process entry
        processes[pid].state = PROC_RUNNING
        processes[pid].ptbr = ptbr_val
        processes[pid].parent_tid = current_thread
        processes[pid].exit_code = 0
        processes[pid].main_tid = tid
        processes[pid].name = name

        // Link thread to process
        threads[tid].pid = pid
        pid

Create a process but leave its thread suspended (STATE_SUSPENDED).
The caller must call resume_process(pid) after setup is complete.
Unlike create_process, the thread is never enqueued — no race.

    create_process_suspended(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, name: string, ptbr_val: int) -> int
        // Allocate PID
        var pid = -1
        var i = 1
        while i < MAX_PROCESSES
            if processes[i].state == PROC_FREE
                pid = i
                break
            i += 1
        if pid < 0
            return -1

        // Create thread slot without enqueuing
        asm("cli")
        ensure_queues_init()
        var idx = -1
        i = 0
        while i < thread_count
            if threads[i].state == STATE_TERMINATED
                idx = i
                break
            i += 1
        if idx < 0
            if thread_count >= MAX_THREADS
                asm("sti")
                return -1
            idx = thread_count
            thread_count += 1
        // Build stack frame with the child's PTBR so writes go to the
        // correct physical pages (SSP is in program-mapped region)
        vm_set_ptbr(ptbr_val)
        val sp = build_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, ssp)
        vm_set_ptbr(KERNEL_L1_BASE)
        threads[idx].ssp = sp
        threads[idx].state = STATE_SUSPENDED
        threads[idx].wake_tick = 0
        threads[idx].name = name
        threads[idx].priority = 0
        threads[idx].join_target = -1
        threads[idx].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
        threads[idx].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
        threads[idx].ctx_switches = 0
        threads[idx].cpu_ticks = 0
        threads[idx].consec_quanta = 0
        threads[idx].notify_value = 0
        threads[idx].notify_pending = 0
        threads[idx].base_priority = 0
        threads[idx].blocked_on = 0
        threads[idx].ptbr = ptbr_val
        threads[idx].pid = 0
        if current_thread >= 0
            threads[idx].uid = threads[current_thread].uid
        else
            threads[idx].uid = 0
        asm("sti")
        val tid = idx

        // Set up process entry
        processes[pid].state = PROC_RUNNING
        processes[pid].ptbr = ptbr_val
        processes[pid].parent_tid = current_thread
        processes[pid].exit_code = 0
        processes[pid].main_tid = tid
        processes[pid].name = name

        // Link thread to process
        threads[tid].pid = pid
        pid

Resume a suspended process (created with create_process_suspended).

    resume_process(pid: int)
        if pid > 0
            if pid < MAX_PROCESSES
                val tid = processes[pid].main_tid
                resume_thread(tid)

    reap_process(pid: int)
        vm_free_process_pt(processes[pid].ptbr)
        processes[pid].state = PROC_FREE

    waitpid_current(pid: int)
        if pid < 0
            return
        if pid >= MAX_PROCESSES
            return
        if processes[pid].state == PROC_ZOMBIE
            reap_process(pid)
            return
        if processes[pid].state == PROC_FREE
            return
        // Block on the process's main thread
        val tid = processes[pid].main_tid
        if threads[tid].state == STATE_TERMINATED
            reap_process(pid)
            return
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_JOINING
        threads[current_thread].join_target = tid
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0

    getpid_current() -> i64
        threads[current_thread].pid

Kill a process by PID. Terminates its main thread and marks
the process as zombie. Returns 0 on success, -1 on error.

    kernel_kill_process(pid: int) -> int
        if pid <= 0
            return -1
        if pid >= MAX_PROCESSES
            return -1
        if processes[pid].state != PROC_RUNNING
            return -1

        val tid = processes[pid].main_tid
        threads[tid].state = STATE_TERMINATED

        // Free pages and reap immediately
        reap_process(pid)

        // Wake threads joining on this thread
        var i = 0
        while i < thread_count
            if threads[i].state == STATE_JOINING
                if threads[i].join_target == tid
                    threads[i].state = STATE_READY
                    threads[i].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
                    enqueue(threads[i].priority, i)
            i += 1
        0

Returns next thread's SSP, or 0 if no threads are READY.
When 0 is returned, the assembly caller does wfi + retries.

Steps:
1. Increment tick counter
2. Save current thread's SSP and accumulate CPU tick
3. Handle quantum expiry (move to back of queue if expired)
4. Check watchdog (terminate thread if over limit)
5. Fire any expired software timers
6. Wake any blocked threads whose sleep time has expired
7. Pick the highest-priority non-empty queue and dequeue its front
8. Return its SSP, or 0 if all queues are empty


    schedule(current_ssp: i64) -> i64
        ticks += 1

        // Save current thread (skip when idle, current_thread == -1)
        if current_thread >= 0
            threads[current_thread].ssp = current_ssp
            threads[current_thread].cpu_ticks += 1
            if threads[current_thread].state == STATE_RUNNING
                // Decrement quantum and handle expiry
                threads[current_thread].quantum -= 1
                threads[current_thread].state = STATE_READY
                if threads[current_thread].quantum <= 0
                    // Full quantum consumed without voluntary yield
                    threads[current_thread].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
                    threads[current_thread].consec_quanta += 1
                    // Watchdog check
                    if watchdog_limit > 0
                        if threads[current_thread].consec_quanta >= watchdog_limit
                            threads[current_thread].state = STATE_TERMINATED
                // Re-enqueue if still ready (watchdog may have terminated)
                if threads[current_thread].state == STATE_READY
                    enqueue(threads[current_thread].priority, current_thread)

        // Wake threads whose sleep/timeout has expired (heap-based, O(log n))
        // Uses lazy deletion: threads woken early (e.g., by IPC) are skipped on pop
        while _sq_len > 0
            val tid = _sq_peek()
            if threads[tid].wake_tick > ticks
                break  // earliest deadline not yet reached
            val _ = _sq_pop()
            // Lazy deletion: skip if thread was already woken by another path
            if threads[tid].state == STATE_BLOCKED
                threads[tid].state = STATE_READY
                threads[tid].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
                enqueue(threads[tid].priority, tid)
            else if threads[tid].state == STATE_SEND_BLOCKED
                if threads[tid].wake_tick > 0
                    // IPC send timeout expired — wake with -2 in saved r1
                    syscall_return_for(tid, -2)
                    threads[tid].state = STATE_READY
                    threads[tid].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
                    threads[tid].wake_tick = 0
                    enqueue(threads[tid].priority, tid)

        // Find highest-priority non-empty queue (lowest number)
        var pri = 0
        while pri < NUM_PRIORITIES
            if (ready_mask & (1 << pri)) != 0
                val next = dequeue(pri)
                if next != QUEUE_EMPTY
                    current_thread = next
                    threads[next].state = STATE_RUNNING
                    threads[next].ctx_switches += 1
                    total_ctx_switches += 1
                    vm_set_ptbr(threads[next].ptbr)
                    return threads[next].ssp
            pri += 1

        // No ready threads — return 0 to signal idle
        current_thread = -1
        vm_set_ptbr(KERNEL_L1_BASE)
        0

Called by boot.asm after kernel_main() returns, so boot can
context-switch into the first thread. Dequeues from the
highest-priority non-empty queue.

    first_thread_ssp() -> i64
        // Dequeue the highest-priority thread
        var pri = 0
        while pri < NUM_PRIORITIES
            if (ready_mask & (1 << pri)) != 0
                val next = dequeue(pri)
                if next != QUEUE_EMPTY
                    current_thread = next
                    threads[next].state = STATE_RUNNING
                    threads[next].ctx_switches += 1
                    total_ctx_switches += 1
                    vm_set_ptbr(threads[next].ptbr)
                    return threads[next].ssp
            pri += 1
        0

The callback is a function pointer (int) that will be called
after delay ticks. One-shot: fires once then deactivates.

    timer_arm(delay: int, callback: i64) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < MAX_TIMERS
            if timers[i].active == 0
                timers[i].active = 1
                timers[i].fire_tick = ticks + delay
                timers[i].callback = callback
                return i
            i += 1
        -1

    timer_cancel(id: int)
        if id >= 0
            if id < MAX_TIMERS
                timers[id].active = 0

Terminates every thread and sets current_thread to -1.
The CPU will enter the idle loop and halt once the cycle
limit is reached (or wfi forever in production).

    kernel_panic()
        var i = 0
        while i < thread_count
            threads[i].state = STATE_TERMINATED
            i += 1
        current_thread = -1
        init_queues()

Reads the value at the base of the thread's user stack (the
address passed as usp during create_thread). If it still equals
STACK_CANARY, the stack has not overflowed.

    check_stack_canary(id: int) -> int
        // usp was stored in the initial stack frame — we need to
        // recover it. For now, check the canary at a known location.
        // The canary is at the usp address passed to create_thread.
        // We don't store usp separately, so this needs the caller
        // to pass the original usp.
        0

    check_stack_at(addr: i64) -> int
        var p: *int = *int(addr)
        if *p == STACK_CANARY
            return 1
        0

Sets state to STATE_SUSPENDED. The thread will not be scheduled
until resume_thread is called. Does not affect the thread's
stack or context -- it can be resumed exactly where it left off.

    suspend_thread(id: int)
        if threads[id].state != STATE_TERMINATED
            if threads[id].state != STATE_SUSPENDED
                threads[id].state = STATE_SUSPENDED

    resume_thread(id: int)
        if threads[id].state == STATE_SUSPENDED
            threads[id].state = STATE_READY
            threads[id].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
            enqueue(threads[id].priority, id)

    kernel_tls_set(packed: int)
        val slot = (packed >> 24) & 0xFF
        val value = packed & 0xFFFFFF
        tls[current_thread * TLS_SLOTS + slot] = value

    kernel_tls_get(slot: int) -> int
        tls[current_thread * TLS_SLOTS + slot]

    kernel_set_idle_hook(hook: i64)
        idle_hook = hook

    kernel_set_tick_hook(hook: i64)
        tick_hook = hook

Sets the target thread's notify_value and marks it as pending.
If the target is waiting for a notification (STATE_NOTIFY_WAIT),
it is woken and re-enqueued.

    notify_send(target_id: int, value: int)
        threads[target_id].notify_value = value
        threads[target_id].notify_pending = 1
        val st = threads[target_id].state
        if st == STATE_NOTIFY_WAIT
            threads[target_id].state = STATE_READY
            threads[target_id].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
            enqueue(threads[target_id].priority, target_id)
        else if st == STATE_RECV_NOTIFY_BLOCKED
            // Wake with -2 to indicate notification (not IPC message)
            syscall_return_for(target_id, -2)
            threads[target_id].state = STATE_READY
            threads[target_id].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
            enqueue(threads[target_id].priority, target_id)

If a notification is already pending, returns immediately.
Otherwise sets state to STATE_NOTIFY_WAIT and the scheduler
will skip this thread until notify_send wakes it.

    notify_wait_current()
        if threads[current_thread].notify_pending == 1
            return
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_NOTIFY_WAIT
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0

    notify_read(id: int) -> int
        val v = threads[id].notify_value
        threads[id].notify_pending = 0
        threads[id].notify_value = 0
        v

Uses notify_value as the event bits. wait_all=1 means all bits
in mask must be set. wait_all=0 means any bit in mask suffices.
The caller should check the bits after waking (Mesa semantics).

Event groups are per-thread -- each thread's notify_value doubles
as its event bits. Other threads set bits via notify_send.

    event_wait_current(mask: int, wait_all: int)
        if wait_all != 0
            if (threads[current_thread].notify_value & mask) == mask
                return
        else
            if (threads[current_thread].notify_value & mask) != 0
                return
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_NOTIFY_WAIT
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0

    event_set_bits(target_id: int, bits: int)
        threads[target_id].notify_value = threads[target_id].notify_value | bits
        threads[target_id].notify_pending = 1
        if threads[target_id].state == STATE_NOTIFY_WAIT
            threads[target_id].state = STATE_READY
            threads[target_id].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
            enqueue(threads[target_id].priority, target_id)

    event_clear_bits(target_id: int, bits: int)
        threads[target_id].notify_value = threads[target_id].notify_value & ~bits

Priority inheritance support. PIMutex is a kernel-managed mutex
that prevents priority inversion: when a high-priority thread
blocks on a mutex held by a low-priority thread, the holder is
temporarily boosted to the blocker's priority.

PIMutex struct layout (3 fields, each int-sized):
- offset 0: owner -- thread ID of holder (-1 = unlocked)
- offset 8: wait_head -- first waiting thread index (QUEUE_EMPTY = none)
- offset 16: wait_tail -- last waiting thread index


    struct PIMutex
        owner: int
        wait_head: int
        wait_tail: int

    pimutex_init_kernel(m: *PIMutex)
        m.owner = -1
        m.wait_head = QUEUE_EMPTY
        m.wait_tail = QUEUE_EMPTY

remove_from_queue -- Remove a specific thread from a priority run queue.
Needed when boosting a READY thread's priority: remove from old queue,
re-enqueue at new priority.

    remove_from_queue(pri: int, idx: int)
        if queue_head[pri] == idx
            queue_head[pri] = threads[idx].next
            if queue_head[pri] == QUEUE_EMPTY
                queue_tail[pri] = QUEUE_EMPTY
                ready_mask = ready_mask & ~(1 << pri)
            threads[idx].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
            return
        var prev = queue_head[pri]
        while prev != QUEUE_EMPTY
            if threads[prev].next == idx
                threads[prev].next = threads[idx].next
                if queue_tail[pri] == idx
                    queue_tail[pri] = prev
                threads[idx].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
                if queue_head[pri] == QUEUE_EMPTY
                    ready_mask = ready_mask & ~(1 << pri)
                return
            prev = threads[prev].next

boost_thread_priority -- Boost a thread's effective priority.
Handles the thread regardless of state:
- RUNNING: just change priority (takes effect at next schedule)
- READY: move between run queues
- MUTEX_WAIT: change priority and propagate to the mutex owner
    (transitive inheritance)


    boost_thread_priority(id: int, new_pri: int)
        if new_pri >= threads[id].priority
            return
        val old_pri = threads[id].priority
        threads[id].priority = new_pri
        if threads[id].state == STATE_READY
            remove_from_queue(old_pri, id)
            enqueue(new_pri, id)
        else if threads[id].state == STATE_MUTEX_WAIT
            if threads[id].blocked_on != 0
                var bm: *PIMutex = *int(threads[id].blocked_on)
                if bm.owner >= 0
                    boost_thread_priority(bm.owner, new_pri)

If the mutex is free, acquire immediately. If held, boost the
owner's priority if needed, add ourselves to the wait queue,
and block with STATE_MUTEX_WAIT. The scheduler will not touch
us until pimutex_unlock_kernel wakes us.

    pimutex_lock_kernel(m: *PIMutex)
        if m.owner == -1
            m.owner = current_thread
            return
        val owner = m.owner
        if threads[current_thread].priority < threads[owner].priority
            boost_thread_priority(owner, threads[current_thread].priority)
        threads[current_thread].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
        if m.wait_head == QUEUE_EMPTY
            m.wait_head = current_thread
            m.wait_tail = current_thread
        else
            threads[m.wait_tail].next = current_thread
            m.wait_tail = current_thread
        threads[current_thread].state = STATE_MUTEX_WAIT
        threads[current_thread].blocked_on = i64(m)
        threads[current_thread].consec_quanta = 0

Restores the caller's priority to base_priority. If there are
waiters, transfers ownership to the first waiter (FIFO order)
and wakes it. The scheduler will then pick the highest-priority
runnable thread.

    pimutex_unlock_kernel(m: *PIMutex)
        val me = current_thread
        threads[me].priority = threads[me].base_priority
        if m.wait_head == QUEUE_EMPTY
            m.owner = -1
            return
        val waiter = m.wait_head
        m.wait_head = threads[waiter].next
        if m.wait_head == QUEUE_EMPTY
            m.wait_tail = QUEUE_EMPTY
        threads[waiter].next = QUEUE_EMPTY
        m.owner = waiter
        threads[waiter].state = STATE_READY
        threads[waiter].blocked_on = 0
        threads[waiter].quantum = DEFAULT_QUANTUM
        enqueue(threads[waiter].priority, waiter)
